Z||000022||ChopTop||11-26-2001||03:49 PM||||I am looking into buying a fiero but before I doI wanted to know why they stopped making them and what are some problems that occur on the car alot<P>The readers digest version; GM lacked vision and balls.  As far as frequent Fiero problems, headlamps, clutch, and spongy brakes seems to be the most frequent complaints.   The most rust prone area is behind the rear wheels.  To check, open deck lid, pull back carpeted trunk liner on both the left and right side.  On six cylinders the rear manifold sometimes develops leaks due to rusty bolts that have broken off.  <BR>All problems can be fixed by you or a competent mechanic.  Remember you're looking at a 18 to 12 year old car. <BR>Best advice is to take your time and look for a Fiero that has been well maintained.  Even though it may be tempting to pick up a cheap Fiero you will probably end up spending more money in the long run. Fiero car clubs are good resources to find well maintained Fiero's.  But always remember it's buyers beware when purchasing a used car.  Good luck||||reg||1
